package cajeta.lang;

import cajeta.error.NoOptionalValueException;

/**
 * Value-typed sum: present or empty. Stack-allocated by default;
 * `T` may be any type — primitive, class reference, struct, even
 * another Optional.
 *
 * ```cajeta
 * Optional<int32> hit = stack Optional<int32>(true, 42);
 * Optional<int32> miss = stack Optional<int32>(false, 0);
 * if (hit.isPresent()) {
 *     int32 v = hit.get();          // 42
 * }
 * if (miss.isEmpty()) {
 *     int32 v = miss.orElse(-1);    // -1
 * }
 * ```
 *
 * ## v1 surface
 *
 * Minimal Java-style core: construction (via ctor for now;
 * Some/None static factories land once generic-static-call syntax
 * is wired), inspection (isPresent/isEmpty), extraction (get/
 * orElse). The lambda-taking combinators (map/filter/flatMap/fold/
 * orElseGet/orElseThrow/ifPresent/inspect) land in P6.3 once
 * function-typed parameters inside generic stdlib classes are
 * verified.
 *
 * ## get() on None
 *
 * Throws `NoOptionalValueException` (recoverable — an unwrap miss is
 * catchable; Unrecoverable is reserved for panic). Guard with
 * isPresent()/isEmpty() or use orElse() when a fallback exists.
 *
 * ## Storage
 *
 * Same semantics as any other class in the unified-class model. Use
 * `heap Optional<T>(...)` for a heap-allocated instance or
 * `stack Optional<T>(...)` for a stack-resident one (with the usual
 * lifetime rules).
 *
 * ## Stream integration (deferred)
 *
 * specification/lang/stream/Streams.md commits to Optional extending `Stream`<T> with
 * single-shot next(); that lands when `Stream`<T> itself does (P6.4+).
 */
public final class Optional<T> {
    boolean present;
    T value;

    /**
     * Build an Optional directly. Pass `present == true` with the held
     * value, or `present == false` for an empty one (the `value` slot is
     * ignored when empty, but still consumed — pass a zero/default). The
     * `Some`/`None` static factories supersede this once generic-static-call
     * syntax lands.
     *
     * ```cajeta
     * Optional<int32> some = stack Optional<int32>(true, 42);
     * Optional<int32> none = stack Optional<int32>(false, 0);
     * if (some.isPresent()) {
     *     int32 v = some.get();      // 42
     * }
     * int32 fallback = none.orElse(-1); // -1
     * ```
     *
     * @EntryPoint
     */
    public Optional(boolean present, T value) {
        this.present = present;
        this.value #= value;
    }

    /**
     * Empty-case constructor: `present` only; the `value` slot keeps its
     * zero-init. Replaces the two-arg `(false, null)` idiom, which never
     * resolved when `T` was a primitive (a `null` arg cannot match a scalar
     * value formal) and silently relied on the pre-ctor memset — the exact
     * footgun the no-matching-constructor error now rejects.
     *
     * @EntryPoint
     */
    public Optional(boolean present) {
        this.present = present;
    }

    @Inline
    public boolean isPresent() {
        return this.present;
    }

    @Inline
    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return !this.present;
    }

    /**
     * Extract the value. Throws `NoOptionalValueException` on empty
     * (optional-absence spec §2) — catchable by type or via
     * `catch (Throwable t)`; guard with isPresent() or use orElse().
     *
     * Returns a **borrow**: ownership stays with this Optional, which frees
     * the value if it holds title. Read it, copy it, or lend it onward — do
     * not write `#` on the result. When the caller genuinely needs the title
     * (the value must outlive this Optional), use [`take`](#take).
     */
    @Inline
    public T get() {
        if (!this.present) {
            throw heap NoOptionalValueException("Optional.get: unwrap of an empty Optional (guard with isPresent() or use orElse())");
        }
        return this.value;
    }

    /**
     * Extract the value **with its title**, leaving this Optional empty.
     *
     * The owned counterpart to [`get`](#get). `get()` hands back a borrow,
     * so `#opt.get()` is a lie — it mints a second owner for a value this
     * Optional still frees. That is a real double-free, and both stdlib
     * occurrences were latent until the transfer-of-a-borrow check learned
     * to see call arguments (stdlib-ownership-convention U2).
     *
     * Where the value arrived by transfer — `Channel.receive` returns
     * `stack Optional<T>(true, #item)` — this is how the title continues on
     * to its next owner. `present` is cleared so the now-borrowed slot is
     * not read again, and the extraction itself decays the slot's ownership
     * bit, so this Optional's drop no longer frees what the caller took.
     *
     * Throws `NoOptionalValueException` on empty, like `get()`. Panics if
     * the slot holds no title — i.e. the value was LENT in (constructed
     * without `#`), so there is no title to take; use `get()` for that.
     */
    public #T take() {
        if (!this.present) {
            throw heap NoOptionalValueException("Optional.take: unwrap of an empty Optional (guard with isPresent() or use orElse())");
        }
        this.present = false;
        return #this.value;
    }

    /** Returns the held value when present, otherwise `fallback`. Never throws. */
    @Inline
    public T orElse(T fallback) {
        if (this.present) {
            return this.value;
        }
        return fallback;
    }
}
